            {
              "id": "af83af41-ed4e-351c-a837-7e679decbf14",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards due to their inherent risks.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, there is no specific C function identified. However, the code seems to be related to error handling or logging, which can often involve unsafe C functions like `sprintf`, `strcpy`, etc.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this type of vulnerability, the general advice is to avoid using unsafe C functions and instead use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library or other libraries. For example, instead of `strcpy`, use `strncpy` or better yet, use C++ strings and their methods.\n\nAlso, always validate and sanitize inputs before processing them. Never trust user input blindly. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWithout the complete context of the code, it's hard to provide a specific fix. However, if we assume that `_err` is a string that is being copied or formatted, a safer alternative could be:\n\n```cpp\nstd::string errno;\nerrno = std::to_string(_err);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet provided does not clearly indicate any specific library dependencies. However, if the `_err` is an error code from a system call or library function, then the relevant system or library headers would be needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
