              "id": "39d6860f-d5aa-3a4e-ab40-2761cbd827da",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file path or mode is not properly validated or controlled.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, or to use these functions in a way that their risks are mitigated. For example, instead of using `fopen`, consider using `fopen_s` which is a safer version of `fopen`. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\nerrno_t err;\nFILE* fp;\n\nerr = fopen_s(&fp, \"inputlu.data\", \"r\");\nif (err == 0) {\n    printf(\"The file 'inputlu.data' was opened\\n\");\n} else {\n    printf(\"The file 'inputlu.data' was not opened\\n\");\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdio.h` for `fopen`, `printf` and `FILE`\n- `errno.h` for `errno_t`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
