            {
              "id": "2855470e-cd7f-3e60-9754-620d842b13cb",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" is a vulnerability that arises when a C++ program uses certain C library functions that are considered unsafe. These functions are prohibited because they can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. \n\nIn this specific case, the function `atoll()` is used, which converts a string to a long long integer. This function does not perform any error checking, and if the string cannot be converted into a long long integer, it could lead to undefined behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using C library functions that do not perform error checking or can lead to security vulnerabilities. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `atoll()` function with `std::stoll()`, which is a safer alternative provided by the C++ Standard Library. `std::stoll()` throws an exception if the conversion cannot be performed, which allows for proper error handling.\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\nint main(int argc, char* argv[]) {\n    if (argc > 1) {\n        try {\n            long long num = std::stoll(argv[1]);\n        } catch (std::invalid_argument& e) {\n            // Handle error\n        } catch (std::out_of_range& e) {\n            // Handle error\n        }\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`: This is a standard C++ library for string manipulation.\n- `<cstdlib>`: This is a standard C++ library that includes functions from the C standard library, including `atoll()`.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
