            {
              "id": "d1f4232c-1743-3ce5-a4cc-669d0b70adfa",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often older, and they do not perform any kind of input validation or bounds checking, which can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows.\n\nIn this specific case, the function `getenv(\"MP_SET_NUMTHREADS\")` is used, which can lead to potential security risks. The `getenv()` function is used to get the value of an environment variable and it returns a pointer to the value string. If an attacker can control the environment in which your program runs, they can modify the environment variable to point to malicious code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking and input validation. In this case, consider using a secure method to retrieve environment variables.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `getenv()`, consider using a method that validates the input. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    const char* value = std::getenv(\"MP_SET_NUMTHREADS\");\n    if (value == nullptr) {\n        std::cout << \"MP_SET_NUMTHREADS is not set.\\n\";\n    } else {\n        // Perform validation on value before using it\n        std::cout << \"MP_SET_NUMTHREADS is set to \" << value << '\\n';\n    }\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we first check if the environment variable is set. If it is not, we output a message and do not use the value. If it is set, we perform validation on the value before using it.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- cstdlib\n- iostream\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            }
